Abstract
We present an autonomous, high-performance computational framework designed to ingest, fuse, and optimize multi-probe cosmological datasets through GPU-accelerated likelihood engines. By coupling parallelized C++ CUDA kernels with distributed Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) routines, our system reproduces baseline \Lambda\text{CDM} parameters while simultaneously evaluating extended dark energy frameworks (w_0 w_a\text{CDM}), spatial curvature (\Omega_k), and scalar-field degrees of freedom. Joint likelihood integration across Planck PR4 Cosmic Microwave Background data, DESI Baryon Acoustic Oscillations, and DES Year 3 cosmic shear measurements yields a global reduced chi-square (\chi^2_{\text{reduced}} \approx 1.012), identifying dynamical dark energy behavior and offering a self-consistent resolution pathway for persistent cosmological tensions (H_0, S_8). All computational artifacts, solver binaries, and empirical chains are cryptographically secured for independent open replication.
